@*
    A field whose values are keys in a namespace outside the knowledge folders:
    `feature-flag`, and `roadmap`.

    Neither names a word this repository uses and neither addresses a chapter. A
    feature flag is an identifier in the application's own catalog; a roadmap item is
    a slug the plan holds. Something outside these files answers to the key, which is
    the fact the treatment has to carry, and it is what separates these from
    MetadataValueList's plain strings and from MetadataReferenceList's addresses.

    Drawn in the feature-badge family — `.badge--feature`, the rule DEV and BETA
    already wear. That family is the product's existing visual language for "an
    application feature is involved here", and a flag key is the most literal case of
    it there is.

    A key is not a status, so no slug is passed. `FeatureStatusBadge` derives its
    modifier from the value, which is right for a two-word vocabulary and wrong for an
    identifier: `feature-flag: inbox-pane` would emit `.badge--feature-inbox-pane`, a
    class no stylesheet defines and none ever will. The family without the per-value
    tone is the honest half of it — the shape says which family, and the text says
    which key.

    Nothing here is clickable. Filtering by a key is a job for a surface that has a
    list to filter, and a badge that took focus to do nothing would be worse than a
    label.

    One component for the two fields, so they cannot drift apart. `roadmap` is drawn
    in this treatment without a story of its own on purpose — the field is out of the
    storybook for now and no file in this repository writes it, so the alternative was
    a second component nobody could look at.
*@

@foreach (var key in Keys)
{
    <Badge Kind="feature" CssClass="@KeyCssClass">@key</Badge>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The keys, in the order the field listed them. Empty draws nothing;
    /// whether the row belongs is the caller's question.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<string> Keys { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>A modifier on each badge, on top of the classes Badge carries — for
    /// a host that draws one of these fields differently from the other.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? KeyCssClass { get; set; }
}
